The Impact Of Globalisation On Poverty
Arguments That Globalisation Has Had A Positive Impact On Poverty
Economic Globalisation Leads To Economic Growth
● A key argument in favour of globalisation is its contribution to global economic growth,
which leads to poverty reduction. Globalisation has facilitated trade liberalisation, expanded
markets, and increased foreign direct investment (FDI), which in turn has boosted
industrialisation and job creation, particularly in developing economies.
○ Between 2000 and 2024, the global economy has nearly tripled in size, with nominal
global GDP increasing from about $33 trillion in 2000 to approximately $104 trillion
by 2023, according to data from the World Bank and IMF.
● Increased global trade and investment have allowed countries to grow their GDP,
particularly by using cheap labour as a competitive advantage to mass produce goods, and
poverty rates have fallen in regions integrating into the global economy.
○ According to World Bank data, extreme poverty rates globally have dropped from
35% in 1990 to 9% in 2019.
● In 2024, for example, Indonesia recorded its lowest ever rate of extreme poverty at 0.83%,
along with its lowest ever rate of poverty at 9.03%. This has largely been attributed to
economic globalisation and the country’s integration into global supply chains, including in
the technological sector and through investment from China’s Belt and Road Initiative.
○ This is therefore a good example of how economic globalisation can reduce poverty,
as access to the global market often produces jobs.
Political Globalisation and International Cooperation Has Reduced Poverty Globally
● Political globalisation has fostered a global commitment to poverty reduction. Institutions
such as the United Nations (UN), the World Bank, and the International Monetary Fund
(IMF) have launched initiatives aimed at reducing poverty and enhancing development.
○ The Millennium Development Goals (MDGs), established in 2000, targeted halving
global poverty by 2015, a goal that was met ahead of schedule.
○ The Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), launched in 2015, aim to eliminate
extreme poverty by 2030.
● The SDGs take an approach to tackling poverty that addresses key structural issues
including inequality, climate change and access to education & healthcare.
Modernisation Theory - Globalisation Has Improved The Global Economy By
Spreading Capitalism and Free Markets
● Modernisation theory argues that globalisation has advanced the global economy and
reduced poverty by spreading capitalism and free market principles.
○ As nations integrate into the global economy, they adopt free market economic
practices such as deregulation, privatisation, and market liberalisation.
○ These reforms attract foreign direct investment (FDI), promote entrepreneurial
activity, and drive technological innovation, which stimulates job creation and
development. This further enables developing countries to move away from reliance
on agriculture and raw materials to industrialised and service-based sectors.
● These reforms have been strongly promoted by the IMF and World Bank, including by
making financial assistance to struggling countries conditional on implementing free market
reforms.
