economy to global markets by adopting
market-oriented policies and encouraging foreign
direct investment (FDI).
Its accession to the World Trade Organisation
(WTO) in 2001 was a turning point. It integrated
into global supply chains, particularly in
manufacturing as it became the world’s
manufacturing hub. As a result, China became
the world’s second-largest economy, with GDP
growth averaging nearly 10% annually over three
decades.
This economic growth lifted more than 800
million people out of extreme poverty, making it
the largest poverty reduction effort in history. The
poverty rate fell from 88% in 1981 to less than
1% by 2019. China’s embrace of globalisation
transformed the country from a largely agrarian
society into an industrial powerhouse,
contributing to significant improvements in living
standards.
India’s economic liberalisation in 1991
represented a decisive break from its earlier
socialist policies. Facing a severe balance of
payments crisis, the Indian government turned to
the IMF and World Bank, which advocated for
reforms such as reducing trade barriers,
deregulating industries, and attracting foreign
direct investment (FDI).
These policies opened India’s economy to global
markets, leading to rapid growth, especially in
sectors like information technology (IT) and
services. India became a global IT hub, with
cities like Bangalore and Hyderabad emerging
as major tech centres.
Over the past couple of decades, India’s GDP
has grown significantly, averaging 6-7%
annually. This growth helped reduce extreme
poverty from 45% in 1993 to around 6% by
2024, according to World Bank data.
