Arguments That Globalisation Has Had A Negative Impact On Poverty
Economic Globalisation Has Deepened Inequality and Marginalisation
● While globalisation has lifted millions out of poverty, it has also deepened inequality within
and between nations.
● Economic globalisation has led to a rise in high-paying jobs for skilled workers, while wages
for unskilled labour have stagnated or declined.
○ In 2021, the richest 1% of the global population controlled 38% of global wealth,
while the poorest half held less than 2%.
● The urbanisation and industrialisation encouraged by globalisation has also led to
displacement of rural communities as land is repurposed for commercial, industrial, or
agricultural ventures.
○ This has led not only to the destruction of rural ways of life, it has also pushed them
further into poverty, as they are unable to access the benefits of globalisation in the
same way as those who are urban, skilled workers.
○ For example, in India, more than 50 million people have been displaced due to
development projects since independence, with many of these linked to industrial
and infrastructure projects aimed at integrating into the global economy.
Globalisation Has Led to the Exploitation of Labour
● Globalisation has led to a “race to the bottom” in labour standards, where companies move
production to countries with cheaper labour, often resulting in low wages and poor working
conditions that perpetuate poverty and sometimes constitute human rights abuses.
○ This exploitation undermines the potential for long-term poverty reduction, as
workers remain trapped in cycles of poverty despite employment.
○ According to the ILO, about 1.5 billion workers worldwide are in vulnerable
employment, often in precarious jobs created by global supply chains.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 5
● As cheap, manual labour has been outsourced to the ‘global south’, rich Western countries
such as the United States have been able to capture the high value-add parts of global
supply change in order to maintain economic supremacy and higher wages.
Free Market Reforms Led to Poverty and Continued Dependence in Sub-Saharan
Africa
● In Sub-Saharan Africa, free market reforms imposed by the IMF and World Bank under
Structural Adjustment Programs (SAPs) in the mid-late 20th century have deepened
poverty and dependence on external financial aid.
○ These reforms, which included privatisation, deregulation, and reduced public
spending on essential services, were often preconditions for receiving financial
assistance.
● The reforms led to cuts to education and healthcare and, according to dependency theory,
entrenched African nations in exploitative global economic structures, forcing them to
remain dependent on exporting raw materials while importing expensive manufactured
goods.
○ As of 2023, approximately 38% of subsaharan Africa remains in extreme poverty
and the region remains heavily dependent on exports of raw materials, with 75% of
its exports consisting of unprocessed goods like oil and minerals, making it highly
vulnerable to global price fluctuations.
● In Nigeria, SAPs were introduced in 1986, leading to privatisation, deregulation, and
devaluation of the currency. These reforms caused inflation to rise, public services to
deteriorate, and living standards to decline, particularly for the poor.
○ By the early 1990s, poverty had increased from 28% to 66%, as real wages fell and
the cost of living soared, making essential goods unaffordable for many.
