Child Labour In Nestlé and Starbucks’ Supply Chains - Example Of The Negative Impact Of
Globalisation and the ‘Race To The Bottom’:
● In December 2024, the Wall Street Journal and China Labor Watch reported on coffee
farms in Yunnan province, China, that supplied both Nestlé and Starbucks.
○ They found that these farms used child labour and subjected workers to excessive
hours without proper contracts, despite both companies’ commitments to having
ethical supply chains.
● This is a clear example of how economic globalisation can have a negative impact on
workers outside of the West, by creating a “race to the bottom” in labour standards.
○ This is where MNCs outsource production and their supply chains to countries with
the lowest wages and labour standards, as this minimises their costs and
maximises their profits.
The Impact Of Globalisation On The Environment
Arguments That Globalisation Has Had A Positive Impact On The
Environment
Political Globalisation Has Helped Coordinate Action Against Climate Change
● Political globalisation has been important in fostering global cooperation to tackle climate
change. International institutions like the UN and treaties like the Paris Agreement have
brought nations together to commit to reducing carbon emissions.
● The Paris Agreement, adopted in 2015, commits countries to keep the global temperature
rise this century well below 2°C above pre-industrial levels. 196 countries have signed the
agreement, representing nearly the entire world.
○ It also aims to strengthen the ability of countries to deal with the impacts of climate
change and requires nations to submit Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs)
outlining their plans to reduce greenhouse gas emissions.
● There has also been some recognition from developed countries about how they have
caused climate change and that they should assist poorer countries to help tackle it.
○ At the COP26 Summit in Glasgow in 2021 developed countries reaffirmed their
commitment to provide $100 billion annually to support developing nations in
mitigating and adapting to the impacts of climate change.
Economic Globalisation Has Enabled Green Technology and Renewable Energy
● It can be argued that economic globalisation has accelerated the development and
distribution of green technology and renewable energy. As global trade networks expanded,
innovations in renewable energy such as solar panels, wind turbines, and electric vehicles
have been able to spread rapidly across borders.
● Countries like China have become major producers of renewable energy technologies,
driving down costs and making these solutions more accessible worldwide.
○ For example, the global cost of solar energy has dropped by nearly 90% since
2010, largely due to international competition and investment.
○ Foreign direct investment (FDI) and cross-border technology transfer have allowed
many developing countries to leapfrog fossil-fuel dependency and invest directly in
clean energy, contributing to reduced global emissions.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 7
Cultural Globalisation Has Led to Global Social Movements Against Climate Change
● A final argument that globalisation has had a positive impact on the environment is that
cultural globalisation has facilitated widespread awareness of environmental issues.
○ The rapid spread of information through the internet and social media has allowed
environmental movements to gain global traction, giving a voice to marginalised
groups and youth activists, particularly from the Global South, who otherwise might
struggle to be heard on the international stage.
○ These platforms have democratised access to global conversations, enabling
grassroots movements to flourish and increasing public pressure on governments
and corporations to prioritise climate action.
● A notable example is the Fridays for Future movement, started by Swedish climate activist
Greta Thunberg in 2018. Thunberg’s solitary school strikes quickly gained international
attention through social media, inspiring millions of young people to join climate strikes
across the globe. The movement united young people from diverse regions, including
Africa, Latin America, and Asia, in calling for stronger and more immediate action on
climate change.
○ The Fridays for Future movement was influential in pushing for stronger
commitments at major environmental summits, such as the 2019 UN Climate Action
Summit and the COP26 conference. These IGOs were urged to adopt more
ambitious climate policies, such as targeting net-zero emissions and intensifying
global efforts to limit temperature rise.
○ The movement also placed immense pressure on multinational corporations to
enhance their sustainability efforts. Many businesses faced public scrutiny and were
compelled to take more concrete steps to reduce their carbon footprints.
■ Major corporations like Amazon, Microsoft, and Unilever publicly announced
new climate goals, such as achieving carbon neutrality or adopting green
energy initiatives.
■ In many cases, companies recognised that failing to respond to the
demands of this global climate movement would result in reputational
damage and potential loss of market share, especially among younger
consumers who prioritise environmental responsibility.
