Arguments That Globalisation Has Had A Negative Impact On The
Environment
Economic Globalisation Has Led to Increased Carbon Emissions, Pollution,
Deforestation, and Resource Exploitation
● Economic globalisation has significantly contributed to environmental degradation, as the
pursuit of wealth and economic growth by corporations and countries has come at the
expense of the environment.
○ Global supply chains, driven by international trade and foreign direct investment
(FDI), have increased industrial production, leading to higher carbon emissions from
transportation, manufacturing, and energy use.
○ The International Energy Agency (IEA) estimates that between 1990 and 2018, the
global energy sector saw a 50% increase in CO2 emissions, driven by industrial
growth and global trade.
● Corporations, in their quest for profits, frequently relocate production to countries with weak
environmental regulations, where pollution controls are minimal. This has led to extensive
environmental pollution, including air and water contamination.
● Moreover, the increased global demand for raw materials, such as oil, minerals, and timber,
has accelerated resource exploitation and deforestation.
○ A report released in late 2024 revealed that global deforestation continued to
increase across 2023, surging to 6.4 million hectares despite the fact that 140
countries pledged in 2021 to end deforestation by 2030.
■ Countries that saw a particular increase included Indonesia and Bolivia and
the increase was driven by global demand for beef, soy and palm oil in
particular.
○ This is a clear example of the negative impact of economic globalisation on the
environment, as demands from across the world for resources and the potential
profits that countries can make stand in the way of real progress on climate change.
● The global economy’s focus on extractive industries has also driven overfishing, depletion
of soil, and the mining of non-renewable resources, exacerbating environmental damage
and undermining the sustainability of ecosystems.
Political Globalisation Has Been Ineffective in Getting Nation-States to Tackle
Climate Change
● While political globalisation has facilitated international discussions on climate change, it
has largely been ineffective in compelling nation-states to take decisive action. Despite
international agreements such as the Paris Agreement, which commits countries to limit
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 9
global temperature rise, many countries have fallen short of their emission reduction
targets.
○ The nature of international agreements allows for voluntary commitments, and the
lack of enforceable mechanisms has meant that countries can set inadequate goals
or fail to meet their commitments without significant consequences.
○ For instance, as of 2023, several major emitters, including the United States and
China, have failed to meet their Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) under
the Paris Agreement.
■ The U.S. initially committed to reducing its emissions by 26-28% below 2005
levels by 2025. However, political challenges, including the U.S. withdrawal
from the Paris Agreement during both Trump administrations, and ongoing
reliance on fossil fuels, have hindered progress.
● Furthermore, global political structures have been unable to overcome the inherent
tensions between developed and developing nations. Developed nations, historically
responsible for the bulk of greenhouse gas emissions, have been slow to meet their
financial commitments to help poorer countries mitigate and adapt to climate change.
○ Many developing nations argue that these financial commitments are insufficient
and delayed, leaving them vulnerable to the worst effects of climate change. This
failure to foster genuine international cooperation has hindered the effectiveness of
political globalisation in addressing the climate crisis.
Social Movements Have Been Largely Ineffective In Causing Meaningful Changes To
Tackle Climate Change
● Despite global climate protests, governments and corporations continue to prioritise
economic growth over environmental protection, leading to insufficient action on climate
change.
○ Corporate greenwashing, where companies make superficial environmental claims
without substantive changes, has also become more common, with many
businesses making token gestures to satisfy public demands while continuing
harmful practices.
○ For example, BP rebranded itself as “Beyond Petroleum” and launched campaigns
highlighting investments in renewable energy, but the company still allocates the
vast majority of its spending to oil and gas exploration and production.
● A clear example of the gap between social movements’ demands and actual policy
outcomes can be seen in the agreements at COP26 in 2021. Despite intense pressure from
environmental activists who called for more urgent action, the final agreements fell short of
the movement’s key goals.
○ Activists had campaigned for a firm commitment to limit global temperature rise to
1.5°C and called for an immediate phase-out of coal, stronger financial
commitments for climate adaptation in developing countries, and a complete end to
fossil fuel subsidies.
○ However, the Glasgow Climate Pact only included a pledge to “phase down” rather
than “phase out” coal, and the financial aid for developing nations was far below the
requested $100 billion per year.
○ These compromises, particularly around fossil fuels and financial support, were
seen as a disappointment by activists, who viewed the outcomes as insufficient to
meet the scale of the climate crisis.
