The Impact Of Globalisation On Human Rights
Arguments That Globalisation Has Had A Positive Impact On Human Rights
Political Globalisation Has Created International Human Rights Law
● Political globalisation has been instrumental in the development of international human
rights law, establishing frameworks that hold governments and individuals accountable for
human rights abuses.
○ The Universal Declaration of Human Rights (UDHR), adopted by the United Nations
in 1948, which laid the foundation for modern international human rights standards.
○ The Rome Statute of 1998 established the International Criminal Court (ICC), which
prosecutes individuals for crimes such as genocide and crimes against humanity.
○ Additionally, regional human rights instruments, such as the European Convention
on Human Rights (ECHR), have created enforceable legal frameworks, allowing
individuals to bring cases against their governments for human rights violations.
● Institutions like the United Nations Human Rights Council (UNHRC), the European Court of
Human Rights (ECHR), and the International Criminal Court (ICC) have created
mechanisms to protect human rights on a global scale.
○ The UNHRC monitors compliance with international human rights treaties, while the
ICC prosecutes individuals for crimes against humanity, war crimes, and genocide.
■ The UNHRC has conducted investigations into the Rohingya crisis in
Myanmar, documenting extensive abuses against the Rohingya Muslim
minority and holding Myanmar’s government accountable.
○ The ECHR, which covers 46 countries in Europe, ensures the protection of human
rights for over 700 million people.
■ In Oliari v. Italy (2015), the court found that Italy violated the rights of
same-sex couples by not providing legal recognition for their relationships.
● These international legal bodies have helped standardise human rights protections across
borders, increasing accountability for violations and providing recourse for victims. The
creation of these institutions shows how political globalisation has fostered global human
rights governance.
Cultural Globalisation Has Led to Human Rights Norms and Awareness of Violations
● Cultural globalisation has significantly raised awareness of human rights norms and
violations through the spread of information and social movements. Global communication
networks, including the internet and social media, have amplified the voices of marginalised
groups and human rights defenders.
● Awareness campaigns by organisations like Amnesty International and Human Rights
Watch, along with grassroots movements such as the #MeToo and Black Lives Matter
movements have shined a global spotlight on human rights issues.
○ These movements have gained momentum, uniting people from different cultures in
a shared commitment to justice and equality.
● Additionally, real-time coverage of human rights violations, such as in conflict zones or
repressive regimes, has mobilised international responses and support for affected
communities.
○ Real-time footage and reports of human rights violations during the Syrian Civil War,
including the use of chemical weapons against civilians, led to international
condemnation and sanctions, as well as humanitarian efforts to assist the affected
population.
