Arguments That Globalisation Has Had A Negative Impact On Human Rights
Humanitarian Interventions Aren’t Always Effective
● Humanitarian interventions, though often justified on the basis of human rights protection,
can be ineffective and lead to unintended consequences.
● While interventions aim to stop human rights violations, such as genocide or ethnic
cleansing, they can sometimes be driven by political or economic interests rather than
genuine humanitarian concerns.
○ In some cases, human rights are used as a pretext for military invasions or regime
change, with examples like the 2003 Iraq invasion being criticised for ulterior
motives and for involving millions of deaths.
● Moreover, state sovereignty often obstructs effective interventions, as seen in the case of
the Uyghur genocide in China. Despite global outrage over China’s treatment of the Uyghur
population, international intervention is largely blocked due to China’s sovereignty,
economic might and its political power in global institutions like the UN Security Council,
where it can veto any potential actions.
The International Criminal Court Is Flawed
● The International Criminal Court (ICC) has faced significant criticism. One of the primary
concerns is its disproportionate focus on Africa, with the vast majority of cases being from
African nations, despite human rights violations occurring globally.
○ This has led to accusations of bias and selective justice, undermining the court’s
credibility in some regions.
● Additionally, key powerful countries, including the United States, China, and Russia, are not
members of the ICC, limiting its ability to hold major global actors accountable.
● The lack of enforcement mechanisms for bringing individuals to trial, particularly when they
reside in countries that do not recognise the court’s authority, further weakens its
effectiveness.
Economic Globalisation Has Facilitated the Violation of Human Rights
● It can be argued that economic globalisation’s prioritisation of profit has accelerated the
violation of human rights through the exploitation of labour, erosion of minority rights, and
environmental degradation.
● As companies seek to lower production costs, many have outsourced manufacturing to
countries with weak labour protections, leading to unsafe working conditions, forced labour,
and low wages.
○ The Rana Plaza disaster in Bangladesh, where over 1,100 garment workers died in
a factory collapse, exemplifies the human cost of globalised production chains.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 12
● Furthermore, globalisation has led to the marginalisation of indigenous peoples and
minorities, as large-scale industrial projects, such as mining and agriculture, often displace
these communities.
○ In Brazil, large-scale deforestation for soy production and cattle ranching has
displaced indigenous groups in the Amazon, threatening their livelihoods and
cultural heritage.
● Environmental degradation caused by global industries, including deforestation, pollution,
and resource depletion, disproportionately impacts vulnerable populations, violating their
rights to clean water, health, and livelihood.
The Impact Of Globalisation On Conflict
Arguments That Globalisation Has Had A Positive Impact On Conflict
Economic Interdependence Has Reduced Conflict
● Economic globalisation has fostered deeper economic ties between nations, making them
interdependent and reducing the likelihood of conflict.
○ This is also influenced by the fact that global trade facilitates accessing raw
materials from other countries without requiring invasion, as well as the dependence
of countries on the continued flow of goods and services, which war can interupt.
● The European Union (EU) is a key example where economic integration, through trade and
the single market, has helped maintain peace among historically conflicting nations France
and Germany.
Political Globalisation Has Reduced Conflict
● Political globalisation has strengthened international institutions and diplomacy, providing
formal mechanisms for conflict resolution.
● Institutions like the UN and the International Court of Justice (ICJ) facilitate dialogue
between countries to prevent escalation into war. The UN Security Council regularly
imposes sanctions or mediates negotiations to prevent conflicts from spiralling.
○ The UN Security Council imposed sanctions and mediated peace talks during the
Iran-Iraq War in the 1980s, which helped lead to a ceasefire in 1988 through UN
Resolution 598.
● Further, the WTO manages trade disputes diplomatically, preventing economic tensions
from leading to armed conflict. These diplomatic forums offer countries alternative ways to
resolve disputes without resorting to war.
There Have Been Effective UN Interventions to Protect Human Rights
● The United Nations has played a critical role in intervening in conflicts to protect human
rights, especially through peacekeeping missions.
○ In East Timor (1999), UN peacekeepers facilitated the transition to independence
after widespread violence, preventing further human rights abuses.
● Similarly, UN missions in Sierra Leone and Liberia helped end long-standing civil wars,
securing peace agreements and protecting civilians from further violence. These
interventions demonstrate the global community’s ability to coordinate humanitarian efforts
that stabilise regions, protect human rights, and build the foundation for lasting peace.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 13
Arguments That Globalisation Has Had A Negative Impact On Conflict
Political Globalisation Has Often Failed To Prevent Conflict
● Despite the rise of political globalisation, international organisations and diplomatic efforts
have often failed to prevent or resolve major conflicts.
● The ongoing Israel-Gaza conflict and the recent Ukraine war highlight the limitations of
international diplomacy and institutions like the United Nations in effectively mediating or
stopping large-scale conflicts.
○ Despite widespread condemnation and sanctions, international responses have
struggled to bring peace and prevent human rights atrocities, reflecting the
limitations of global governance structures in influencing nation states.
International Humanitarian Interventions Violate National Sovereignty
● International interventions on humanitarian grounds violate national sovereignty and often
have unintended consequences.
● While these interventions are sometimes justified on the grounds of protecting human
rights, they can also provoke conflict by infringing on a nation’s autonomy.
For example, NATO’s intervention in Libya in 2011, though aimed at protecting civilians, resulted in
long-term instability and internal conflict, with critics arguing that it undermined Libya’s sovereignty.
